# -*- coding: utf-8 -*-
from nums import *

add(("title", "Fractional-Order Modified Green–Lindsay Bioheat Thermoelasticity of Layered Tumor Tissue with Temperature-Dependent Blood Perfusion: A Finite Element and Laplace-Domain Study"))
add(("authors", "[Author names, affiliations and corresponding-author e-mail to be inserted]"))
add(("h1u", "Abstract"))
add(("abstract",
 "Thermal therapies deposit heat in a tumour that is surrounded by normal tissue with different thermal, mechanical and vascular properties, and the resulting temperature and stress fields depend on how heat conduction, strain rate and blood perfusion are modelled. "
 "This paper extends the layered tumour and normal tissue slab of Jangid, Singh and Mukhopadhyay [13] in three directions. The strain-rate and temperature-rate operators of the modified Green–Lindsay (MGL) theory are used; the temperature-rate lag in the energy equation is made fractional by a Caputo derivative of order $1+\\alpha$ with $0<\\alpha\\le1$; and blood perfusion depends on temperature through a law that first increases and then shuts down above a critical temperature rise, with an optional linear dependence of conductivity on temperature handled by a Kirchhoff transform. "
 "The model is solved by a Galerkin finite element method with linear elements, a second-order backward-differentiation convolution quadrature for all time derivatives including the fractional one, and Picard iteration for the nonlinear terms. A Legendre wavelet collocation method was not implemented, and the finite element route is the one reported. "
 "For the linear model the exact layered solution is derived in the Laplace domain, where the Caputo term becomes $s^{1+\\alpha}$, and is inverted numerically. For $\\alpha=0.6$ the finite element temperature, displacement and stress agree with it to " + pct(V["H_a0.6_t0.4"]["th"]) + ", " + pct(V["H_a0.6_t0.4"]["u"]) + " and " + pct(V["H_a0.6_t0.4"]["sg"]) + " per cent at $t=0.4$, and a smooth-data test shows second-order convergence in time. For $\\alpha=1$ the discontinuous thermal front limits the accuracy to several per cent. "
 "In the source dimensionless scaling the perfusion and laser numbers are of order $10^{-13}$ and $10^{-12}$, so they are negligible, and the perfusion studies use an explicitly rescaled perfusion number. Decreasing $\\alpha$ from 1 to 0.45 removes the sharp thermal front, lowers the tumour-edge stress peak from " + f2(edge_peak("base")[0]) + " to " + f2(edge_peak("alpha_0.45")[0]) + " and lets the temperature reach $x=0.5$ much earlier. "
 "The field does not vanish beyond the interface once $t$ exceeds about " + f3(th_arr) + ". All results are dimensionless model predictions and carry no clinical claim."))
add(("kw", "fractional heat conduction; modified Green–Lindsay thermoelasticity; bioheat transfer; temperature-dependent blood perfusion; layered tissue; Caputo derivative; convolution quadrature; finite element method"))

H1("1. Introduction")
P("Heating of living tissue by lasers, focused ultrasound or other sources is used to destroy tumours, and the design of such treatments needs predictions of the temperature and of the thermomechanical stress that the heating produces. The classical model is the Pennes bioheat equation, in which Fourier conduction is combined with a heat sink proportional to the blood perfusion rate and to the local temperature rise. "
  "That model is parabolic and therefore gives an infinite speed of heat propagation. For very short heating times, or for tissue with a microstructure that delays the heat flux, a finite speed of thermal waves and a relaxation of the heat flux are introduced in the form of dual-phase-lag, Cattaneo–Vernotte and Moore–Gibson–Thompson (MGT) bioheat models. "
  "Because heated tissue also expands, the temperature field is coupled to the displacement field, and generalised thermoelasticity theories supply the framework for this coupling. Reviews of generalised thermoelasticity and of its modified versions are given in [9].")
P("Among the generalised theories, the Green–Lindsay theory introduces two relaxation times through temperature-rate terms in the constitutive law and in the energy equation. Its well known defect is a displacement that can jump at the wavefront. Yu, Xue and Tian (2018) repaired this by adding a strain-rate term that uses the same relaxation time as the temperature rate, and Quintanilla (2018) studied the resulting modified Green–Lindsay (MGL) system. "
  "Recent work has clarified the mathematical significance of the strain-rate and temperature-rate terms [1], their infinite-speed behaviour [2], the fundamental solutions [3] and a Galerkin-type representation [4]. Finite element solutions of MGL problems exist for functionally graded geometries [5] and photothermal problems have been treated within the same theory [6]. "
  "The MGT theory provides a competing third-order description whose line-source and wave properties have been compared with those of MGL [7, 8]. The present paper stays within the MGL family, and the MGT model is used only as a comparison.")
P("The source of this work is the study of Jangid, Singh and Mukhopadhyay [13]. They consider a one-dimensional slab in which a tumour occupies $0<x<h$ and normal tissue occupies $h<x<L$, with a temperature step at the surface of the tumour, a clamped and isothermal far face, continuity of displacement, stress, temperature and heat flux at the interface, and a laser source with an exponential depth profile. "
  "The thermal part is an MGT bioheat equation with a temperature-dependent conductivity that is active in the tumour only, and the system is solved by a Legendre wavelet collocation method. Their reported findings are that the MGT and Lord–Shulman responses are close, that the Biot and Green–Naghdi type III responses are larger, that the variable conductivity matters in the tumour, and that the fields vanish beyond the interface for the times considered.")
P("Three aspects of that model call for extension. First, the elastic part of the source model is classical, whereas the MGL theory supplies the strain-rate regularisation that is known to remove the displacement discontinuity at the front. Second, memory and non-local effects in biological tissue are often described by fractional derivatives, and a Caputo derivative of order $1+\\alpha$ interpolates between the diffusive limit and the integer-order hyperbolic model. "
  "The fractional-order literature in thermoelasticity and bioheat transfer is large, with contributions by Ezzat (2020), Li et al. (2019, 2020), Zhang et al. (2021), Marin et al. (2021), Ragab et al. (2021) and Hobiny et al. (2020), among others, and the MGT bioheat models of Quintanilla (2019) and Singh and co-workers (2023), and the wavelet analysis of Jangid and Mukhopadhyay (2023) are directly related. "
  "These works are named here by author and year only, because their digital object identifiers could not be verified during the preparation of this manuscript. Third, the perfusion rate of living tissue is not constant. It rises with temperature through vasodilation, and it falls when the vasculature is damaged or collapses at higher temperature, which is the physical background of the Weinbaum–Jiji type models and of the observed vascular shutdown in hyperthermia.")
P("The contribution of the paper is a complete, verified computational treatment of this extended problem. The model (Section 2) combines the MGL operators, a fractional temperature-rate lag, a perfusion law with a critical temperature, optional temperature-dependent conductivity and the laser source, and it states exactly which terms are fractional. "
  "The solution (Section 3) is a finite element method with a convolution quadrature in time, together with an independent exact Laplace-domain solution of the linear layered problem that is used for validation. The results (Section 4) contain convergence and limit checks, a comparison of the Pennes, MGT, MGL and fractional MGL models, and studies of the fractional order, the strain-rate lag, the perfusion law, the interface position and the laser source. Section 5 summarises the findings.")
P("Several modelling choices were made for the sake of a definite and testable problem, and they are repeated here because they affect interpretation. The preferred solution method for the extension was a Legendre wavelet collocation method with a Caputo operational matrix, as in the source study. That method was not implemented here; the finite element and convolution quadrature route was chosen instead because it could be validated against an exact solution in pure Python within the available effort. "
  "Next, the source nondimensionalisation makes the length unit about " + g(SC["l"] * 1e9, 3) + " nm and the time unit about " + g(SC["tl"] * 1e12, 3) + " ps, so that the literal perfusion and laser numbers are negligible, and this fact must be understood before any perfusion result is read. "
  "Finally, the relaxation times, the perfusion law and the parameters of the law are illustrative and are not taken from a cited measurement. The numerical results are therefore properties of the model and are expressed in dimensionless form.")
